(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-electrocardiography-quiz"]={"title":"Electrocardiography Quiz 1 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is phase 2 called the plateau?","choices":["All ion movement stops entirely","Calcium entering the cell is matched by potassium moving the other way","Sodium continues to enter at a constant rate","The cell has returned to its resting voltage and remains there"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the calcium entering during this balance is what actually produces contraction, so the flat part of the trace is the working part of the beat. So the voltage holds. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which relationship is checked when assessing P waves on a strip?","choices":["Whether the P wave is taller than the T wave","Whether the P wave falls within the QT interval","Whether there is a P wave for every QRS complex and a QRS for every P wave","Whether the P wave is visible in all twelve leads"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is a two-way check, and each direction fails in a different way, which is what makes the blocks distinguishable. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which structure is described as the third pacemaker, taking over if both the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes fail?","choices":["The bundle of His","The Purkinje network","The left anterior fascicle","The internodal tracts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, at an intrinsic rate of twenty to forty -- slow enough to be a last resort rather than a comfortable substitute. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"The overall vector at a given moment is directed away from a lead. What does that lead record?","choices":["A positive deflection","An isoelectric line","No tracing at all","A negative deflection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the converse holds: toward the lead gives a positive deflection, which is why the same beat looks different in different leads. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How is the ATRIAL rate obtained by the six-second method?","choices":["Count the QRS complexes and multiply by ten","Count the T waves and multiply by ten","Measure the PR interval and divide into sixty","Count the P waves in six seconds and multiply by ten"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and doing both counts separately is what reveals a heart block, where the two rates differ. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Over which part of the action potential does the ABSOLUTE refractory period run?","choices":["From the middle of phase 3 to the end of phase 3","Throughout phase 4","From phase 1 to phase 2 only","From phase 0 to the middle of phase 3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it lasts about a hundred and eighty milliseconds, during which no stimulus of any strength will produce another action potential. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What distinguishes a SEGMENT from an INTERVAL?","choices":["A segment includes a wave; an interval is the flat line between waves","They are two names for the same thing","A segment is the flat line between two waves","A segment is measured in millivolts and an interval in seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An interval is the larger unit, which is why the PR interval contains the PR segment rather than being the same thing. An interval includes at least one wave plus at least one segment. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which six electrocardiographic leads do the four limb electrodes produce?","choices":["Leads I, II and III together with aVR, aVL and aVF","V1 through V6","Leads I, II and III only","aVR, aVL and aVF only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These six are the hexaxial leads, and they are what the hexaxial reference system uses to determine the axis. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What two things does the atrioventricular node do to the impulse passing through it?","choices":["It introduces a short pause and amplifies the signal","It speeds the impulse up and attenuates it","It blocks every second impulse by design","It reverses the direction of the impulse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pause is what lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract, so a delay in the conduction system is doing useful mechanical work. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In which direction does conduction truly travel through the heart?","choices":["Right and up, toward the shoulder","Left and down, toward the anterior chest","Straight downward, in the coronal plane only","Posteriorly, away from the chest wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and this is why lead II sees the beat so well: it looks along that axis. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does the Q wave represent, and why is it negative in lead II?","choices":["Depolarization of the anterior left ventricle, traveling toward lead II","Depolarization of the lateral wall of the left ventricle","Depolarization of the interventricular septum, which travels away from lead II","Repolarization of the ventricles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The direction of that particular wavefront is what makes the deflection downward, and it is not always visible. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What happens during phase 0, the upstroke?","choices":["Fast sodium channels open and sodium rushes into the cell","Calcium channels open and calcium enters the cell","Potassium channels open and potassium leaves the cell","Sodium is actively pumped out of the cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the fastest event in the cycle, and the rapid inward sodium current is what drives the voltage positive. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What happens during phase 3, rapid repolarization?","choices":["Calcium channels close while potassium channels remain open, returning the cell to minus ninety millivolts","Calcium channels open and the voltage rises","Fast sodium channels reopen","The membrane holds at a steady plateau voltage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The inward current stops and the outward one continues unopposed, which is why this phase is the rapid one. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of these is one of the described contiguous groups?","choices":["Leads II, III and aVF","Leads I, II and III","Leads aVR, aVL and aVF","Leads V1, V3 and V5"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The other two groups are V1 to V4, and leads I, aVL, V5 and V6. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What distinguishes the relative from the absolute refractory period?","choices":["In the relative period a sufficiently strong stimulus can produce a response","In the relative period no stimulus of any strength will produce a response","The relative period occurs before the absolute period","The relative period occupies the whole of phase 4"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the dangerous window: the cell is excitable again but not yet fully recovered, so a stimulus arriving there can start something abnormal. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the R to R interval used to assess?","choices":["Rate, regularity","Ventricular repolarization","Atrial depolarization","The isoelectric baseline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One measurement doing three jobs, which is why comparing consecutive R to R intervals is the first thing done to a strip. Heart blocks. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How is regularity determined when it is not obvious by eye?","choices":["By counting the number of P waves in six seconds","By comparing consecutive R to R intervals with calipers","By measuring the QT interval in several leads","By assessing the isoelectric line on the TP segment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The marked paper is the field substitute, and it answers the same question the calipers do. Or a marked sheet of paper. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What limits the use of the three hundred method?","choices":["It only works on a six-second strip","It only works in lead II","It only works at rates above one hundred","It only works if the rhythm is regular"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why regularity is assessed before rate: the answer decides which method may be used. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is distinctive about the bundle of His in a normal heart?","choices":["It is one of several pathways crossing the atrioventricular junction","It paces the heart at sixty to a hundred beats per minute","It is the only electrical pathway crossing from the atria to the ventricles","It lies within the atrial wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Everything above and below has to go through it, which is why a block there separates the two halves of the heart electrically. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Reading left to right across the paper measures time. What does reading up and down measure?","choices":["Rate","Voltage","Time in a second dimension","Conduction velocity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and at standard calibration a small box is a tenth of a millivolt, with a ten millimeter deflection equal to one millivolt. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is happening mechanically during the ST segment?","choices":["The ventricles are filling during diastole","The atria are contracting","The ventricles are holding their contraction to allow emptying","The ventricles are repolarizing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It falls between ventricular depolarization and repolarization, and it is the part of the beat that does the ejecting. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What view of the heart do the precordial leads give?","choices":["A frontal view","A horizontal view","A sagittal view only","No spatial view; they measure rate only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which complements the frontal plane the limb leads provide, so the two sets together look at the heart from two directions. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Prominent U waves are associated with which conditions?","choices":["Hyperkalemia and hypocalcemia","Atrial fibrillation","Hypokalemia, hypercalcemia, prolonged QT and the period after myocardial infarction","Pericardial effusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the electrolyte associations are the practically useful ones, since the wave itself is not fully understood. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A heart is being paced at a rate in the forties, with no sinoatrial activity. Which pacemaker is most likely responsible?","choices":["The sinoatrial node","The atrioventricular node","The Purkinje network","The bundle of His alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Its intrinsic rate is forty to sixty, and it takes over as the backup when the sinoatrial node fails. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which ion entering the cell during the plateau causes contraction?","choices":["Sodium","Calcium","Potassium","Chloride"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the link between the electrical event and the mechanical one, which is why the plateau is where excitation becomes contraction. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the R wave represent?","choices":["Depolarization of the interventricular septum","Depolarization of the lateral wall of the left ventricle","Depolarization of the atria","Depolarization of the anterior left ventricle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is the first positive deflection, following either the PR interval or the Q wave. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A six-second strip contains eight QRS complexes. What is the ventricular rate?","choices":["Eighty beats per minute","Forty-eight beats per minute","Eight beats per minute","One hundred and sixty beats per minute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The six-second method multiplies the count by ten, since six seconds is a tenth of a minute. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does the QRS complex represent, and what should it look like?","choices":["Ventricular repolarization, broad and rounded","Atrial depolarization, narrow and upright","The plateau phase of the action potential","Ventricular depolarization, narrow with sharp points"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with a duration under three small boxes. A wide QRS means the ventricles were not activated through the normal fast pathway. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the hexaxial reference system used to determine?","choices":["The heart rate","The horizontal view of the heart","The normal axis of the heart","The duration of the QT interval"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Six views in one plane are what make an axis calculable at all. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A PR interval measures seven small boxes. How should that be read?","choices":["Normal","Shortened","Uninterpretable without the heart rate","Prolonged, since the normal range is three to five small boxes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The normal interval runs 0.12 to 0.2 seconds, so seven small boxes is beyond it. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
